Spec: primary navigation hover and active states

Behavior

Each nav item is a real <a>. The entire item — including its padding — is the hit area, not just the text.
Hover lightens the item's background and brings the label to full white. It does not move, underline, or resize.
The item for the section you're currently on has a persistent treatment that is clearly different from hover, not just stronger: a left accent bar plus bolder weight. If active and hover look the same, people lose track of where they are.
Keyboard focus renders identically to hover, plus a visible focus ring.
Only the surface tint and text color transition, at 100ms. Nothing shifts position.

Why not underline-on-hover: an underline appears and disappears at the text's height, so it reads as decoration. A full-width background band reads as "this whole strip is a target," which is what you want on a rail with five items.

Sidebar version (dark rail, what's in the mockup)

html
<nav class="flex flex-col">
  <a href="/tables"
     class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 px-3.5 py-2 text-[13px]
            border-l-[3px] border-transparent text-emerald-100/80
            transition-colors duration-100
            hover:bg-white/[0.06] hover:text-white
            focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:bg-white/[0.06]
            focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-inset focus-visible:ring-emerald-400">
    Tables
  </a>

  <!-- active item -->
  <a href="/monitoring" aria-current="page"
     class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 px-3.5 py-2 text-[13px]
            border-l-[3px] border-emerald-400 bg-white/10 text-white font-semibold">
    Monitoring
    <span class="rounded-full bg-red-600 px-1.5 text-[11px] font-bold text-white">2</span>
  </a>
</nav>

Tokens

Rail background: 
#0b3d31. Idle label: emerald-100 at 80% opacity.
Hover surface: white/[0.06]. Active surface: white/10, label full white, font-semibold.
Active accent: border-l-[3px] border-emerald-400. Every item carries border-l-[3px] border-transparent at rest so nothing shifts by 3px when the state changes — this is the detail that's usually missed.
Focus ring must be ring-inset; a normal ring gets clipped by the rail edge.

Top-bar version (if you keep horizontal nav)

Same logic, rotated. The accent moves from the left edge to the bottom edge, and the transparent placeholder border sits on the bottom:

html
<a href="/tables"
   class="px-4 py-3 text-sm border-b-2 border-transparent text-emerald-50/80
          transition-colors duration-100
          hover:bg-white/[0.08] hover:text-white
          focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-inset focus-visible:ring-emerald-300">
  Tables
</a>
<!-- active: border-b-2 border-white bg-white/10 text-white font-semibold -->

Marking the active item server-side (Thymeleaf)

Don't do this in JavaScript. Pass a section attribute from each controller and compare:

html
<a th:href="@{/tables}"
   th:classappend="${section == 'tables'}
     ? 'border-emerald-400 bg-white/10 text-white font-semibold'
     : 'border-transparent text-emerald-100/80 hover:bg-white/[0.06] hover:text-white'"
   th:attr="aria-current=${section == 'tables'} ? 'page' : null"
   class="flex items-center justify-between px-3.5 py-2 text-[13px] border-l-[3px] transition-colors duration-100">
  Tables
</a>

aria-current="page" is what tells a screen reader which section is open — the color alone doesn't.

Two rules to hand over with this

Never rely on color alone for the active state. Weight and the accent bar carry it if color is unavailable.
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { transition-duration: 0.01ms !important } } applies here too.
